# Queue

The generated queue package builds the default queue from QUEUE_* env vars and optional named queues from QUEUE_<NAME>_* env vars.

Usage:

```go
queue := app.Queue()
critical := app.Queues().Critical()

_ = queue
_ = critical
```

The default queue is available from app.Queue(). Named queues are available from app.Queues().<Name>() accessors generated from QUEUE_<NAME>_* scopes.

Named generated accessors such as app.Queues().Critical() do not return errors. They represent generated configuration invariants from the env state present when forj generate --queue last ran. If one of those accessors is missing at runtime, the generated code and the current env configuration are out of sync, so the accessor fails fast.

`QUEUE_SUPPORTED_DRIVERS` controls which queue drivers are generated into the app at compile time. `QUEUE_DRIVER` and `QUEUE_<NAME>_DRIVER` still choose which enabled driver each queue instance uses at runtime.

This is useful when an app owner wants to build and distribute one app binary that can run in different environments. For example, you may want an in-process workerpool queue for development, but Redis in production. Enabling both in `QUEUE_SUPPORTED_DRIVERS` lets the generated app compile with both backends available, while deployment env still decides which one is active.

Example:

```env
QUEUE_SUPPORTED_DRIVERS=workerpool,redis
QUEUE_DRIVER=workerpool
QUEUE_CRITICAL_DRIVER=redis
```

That generates support for both workerpool and Redis, uses workerpool for the default queue, and Redis for the `critical` queue.

Supported env vars by driver:

| Driver | Env Suffix | Value Type | Default |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| all | DRIVER | string | workerpool |
| all | WORKERS | int | 30 |
| all | NAME | string | default for the root queue; resource name for named queues |
| all | SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT | duration | 10s |
| redis | ADDR | host:port | REDIS_HOST:REDIS_PORT or redis:6379 |
| redis | PASSWORD | string | REDIS_PASSWORD |
| redis | DB | int | REDIS_DB or 0 |
| redis | QUEUES | advanced queue=weight map | default=1 |
| redis | SERVER_LOG_LEVEL | string | backend default |
| nats | URL | URL | nats://127.0.0.1:4222 |
| sqs | REGION | string | us-east-1 |
| sqs | ENDPOINT | URL | empty |
| sqs | ACCESS_KEY | string | empty |
| sqs | SECRET_KEY | string | empty |
| rabbitmq | URL | URL | amqp://guest:guest@127.0.0.1:5672/ |
| sqlite | DSN | string | temp sqlite file per queue |
| sqlite | PROCESSING_RECOVERY_GRACE_SECONDS | int seconds | 2 |
| sqlite | PROCESSING_LEASE_NO_TIMEOUT_SECONDS | int seconds | 300 |
| postgres | DSN | string | empty |
| postgres | PROCESSING_RECOVERY_GRACE_SECONDS | int seconds | 2 |
| postgres | PROCESSING_LEASE_NO_TIMEOUT_SECONDS | int seconds | 300 |
| mysql | DSN | string | empty |
| mysql | PROCESSING_RECOVERY_GRACE_SECONDS | int seconds | 2 |
| mysql | PROCESSING_LEASE_NO_TIMEOUT_SECONDS | int seconds | 300 |

Examples:

Default workerpool queue:

```env
QUEUE_SUPPORTED_DRIVERS=workerpool
QUEUE_DRIVER=workerpool
QUEUE_WORKERS=30
QUEUE_NAME=default
```

Named Redis queue instance:

```env
QUEUE_SUPPORTED_DRIVERS=workerpool,redis
QUEUE_CRITICAL_DRIVER=redis
QUEUE_CRITICAL_NAME=critical
```

`QUEUE_DEFAULT_QUEUE` and `QUEUE_<NAME>_DEFAULT_QUEUE` are still accepted as compatibility aliases, but new App configuration should prefer `QUEUE_NAME` and `QUEUE_<NAME>_NAME`.

If a runtime queue selects a driver that is not listed in `QUEUE_SUPPORTED_DRIVERS`, generation fails fast with a validation error.
